<p>&nbsp;Establishing and operating a successful business in Dubai requires more than selecting a company name and obtaining a licence. Entrepreneurs must choose a legal structure that reflects their commercial objectives, understand the requirements of the relevant mainland or free-zone authority, and establish appropriate accounting and financial controls. As the business grows, corporate tax, related-party transactions, transfer pricing, documentation, and regulatory reporting become increasingly important. Advance Pricing Agreements may provide greater certainty for businesses with complex intercompany arrangements, while benchmarking and appropriate transfer-pricing documentation can help support the arm’s length position of related-party transactions. Small Business Relief and industrial development initiatives may also offer valuable support to eligible enterprises, provided that the relevant conditions are met. By combining suitable business structuring, effective financial management, careful tax planning, and ongoing compliance, entrepreneurs and investors can build a stronger foundation for sustainable growth in the UAE.</p>
